## Creating Domain-Specific Error Types

Instead of using `Str` for errors, define custom error enums. This gives you type-safe error handling and clear documentation.

### Defining Error Enums

```xiom
pub type ParseError = enum {
  EmptyInput,
  InvalidChar(pos: Int),
  Overflow,
} derive[Debug]

fn parse_int(s: Str) -> Result[Int, ParseError] {
  if s.len() == 0 { return Err(ParseError.EmptyInput); }
  if s.len() > 10 { return Err(ParseError.Overflow); }
  return Ok(0);  // simplified
}
```

### Matching Custom Errors

```xiom
match parse_int(input) {
  Ok(n) => { io.println("Parsed: " + n); }
  Err(ParseError.EmptyInput) => { io.println("No input provided"); }
  Err(ParseError.InvalidChar(pos)) => { io.println("Bad char at " + pos); }
  Err(ParseError.Overflow) => { io.println("Number too large"); }
}
```

### Error with Context

```xiom
pub type DbError = enum {
  ConnectionFailed(reason: Str),
  QueryFailed(query: Str, code: Int),
  Timeout(seconds: Float64),
} derive[Debug]

fn connect(host: Str) -> Result[Int, DbError] {
  return Err(DbError.ConnectionFailed("host unreachable"));
}
```

### Error Messages from Enums

```xiom
fn ParseError.message(self: &ParseError) -> Str {
  match self {
    EmptyInput => { return "Input is empty"; }
    InvalidChar(pos) => { return "Invalid character at position " + pos; }
    Overflow => { return "Number exceeds maximum value"; }
  }
}

let result = parse_int("");
match result {
  Err(e) => { io.println(e.message()); }
  Ok(_) => { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Composite Errors

Create a top-level error that wraps sub-errors:

```xiom
pub type AppError = enum {
  Parse(inner: ParseError),
  Database(inner: DbError),
  Io(message: Str),
}
```

